we had a bunch of interesting elections  yesterday a very important reminder  useful reminder that we don't just have  a presidential race going on right now  we have 435 House seat elections coming  up in November  many primaries for those happening in  the months leading up we have 35 Senate  elections and the possibility that  Democrats would be able to take over the  Senate we've looked at that and  yesterday there were a bunch of  interesting primaries that I want to  talk about today  not at all surprisingly congresswoman  Alexandria Castillo Cortes had a primary  challenger who she easily defeated the  Challenger was Michele Caruso Cabrera a  CNBC host former Republican running now  as the Democrat and AOC easily won as of  right now looks like AOC had about 73  percent of the vote in that race because  that's such a blue district the primary  is essentially the general election like  there will be a Republican on the ballot  against AOC in November but the fact  that she won the primary all but  guarantees that AOC will get a second  term in the House of Representatives  another race that we were watching very  closely and we'll continue to watch  closely now that we have a primary  winner is the Democratic challenger  against Senate Majority Leader Mitch  McConnell for his Kentucky Senate seat  Mitch McConnell has been one of the most  successful Republicans from the the the  measurement from the standpoint of what  he's been able to achieve as many of you  know a an excellent and effective tool  for the right in getting a bunch of  judges confirmed holding off holding  open a seat on the Supreme Court that  should have been Barack Obama's to  replace with Merrick garland obstructing  Mitch McConnell that seat for long  enough that Donald Trump ended up  getting to choose Neel Gore such an  incredible coup victory for Republicans  horrible for the world horrible for the  country never should have been allowed  to happen but if you're a Republican  certainly Mitch McConnell has been  effective he has a serious primary  challenge and Amy McGrath seemed to be  the leader going  into the primary over the last couple of  weeks there was some energy around  Charles Booker a more progressive  alternative in the Democratic primary  but indeed Amy McGrath has won there  with about 45% of the vote  that's a plurality Charles Booker only  with about 36% of the vote so we will  see Amy McGrath challenged Mitch  McConnell we've said before it's a very  uphill battle but even getting Mitch  McConnell to have a fight even of  forcing Republicans nationally to have  to spend money to defend Mitch  McConnell's seat is great and she could  actually win Amy McGrath was up by one  point a tie within the margin of error  statistically speaking a couple of weeks  ago that's going to be a great one to  watch interesting Republican primary in  North Carolina to mention where a  24-year old guy defeated the Trump  endorsed candidate in the Republican  primary for a House seat his name is  Madison Kath ORN Trump endorsed his  opponent Linda Bennett this is the 11th  congressional district in North Carolina  that's the seat that was formerly held  by Trump's now latest chief of staff  mark Meadows who's a horrible horrible  guy unofficially Cawthorne won very  easily by about a 75 to 25 margin this  is a notable defeat for the Trump  endorsed candidate but it's important to  remember that the guy that won Cawthorne  he also loves Trump is it Trump endorsed  the the his opponent but this is not  like an anti Trump Republican that one  Catherine loves Trump it's just that  Trump picked somebody else so either way  if he wins in November it's still  horrible interestingly he's not even old  enough to serve as of right now but  because he will be 25 by the time he  would be inaugurated he's allowed to be  on the ballot he will go on to face the  Democratic candidate MO Davis and that's  a Republican leaning district so at a  sort of background level at a base level  that that is one that favors Republicans  there are four congressional  candidates who support Medicare for all  who won primaries yesterday Ritchie  Torres Jamaal Bowman Mondt Air Jones and  Dana Balter so that's interesting and  important and we'll see what happens  with their races in November and then a  really interesting story out of Virginia  dr. Cameron Webb  I call him WebMD that's how I refer to  him but officially dr. Cameron Webb he  have won the Democratic primary in  Virginia's fifth District and he will  probably face off against Republican Bob  good in November that would be a really  great seat to turn from red to blue Webb  ran hugely on affordable health care  he's a physician he was leading with 68%  of the vote last I checked so that's a  big win for him that actually is a  traditionally Republican district which  is flippable and Virginia has been a  what I call it Virginia has been a  realistic focus of going blue we've seen  many elements of Virginia go blue over  the last eight 10 years and officially  that race leans Republican but it is a  winnable seat and we'll just have to see  how the campaign shakes out it's a wild  district in that last election cycle  Republican Denver Riggleman won the seat  by about 6 points and then was removed  by the local Republican convention  because he officiated a gay wedding for  two of his old friends and campaign  staffers which is it it Virginia's been  getting pulled blue it is still Virginia  and this stuff still exists there so a  number of interesting races this ties  into how will we vote in November and  the discussions of absentee ballots and  vote by mail we'll be talking about that  later today the desire for scientists  and educators and doctors at least on  the Democratic side it seems partially  to be related to what we've been seeing  with coronavirus and the failed federal  response in the United States and that's  exactly where I want to go next  if you love feeding your intellectual  curiosity but you're always struggling  to find the time check out one of my  all-time favorite apps called blinking  cast lets you consume an entire book on  your way home from work  they take thousands of popular  nonfiction books condense each one into  text or audio that you can get through  in just 15 minutes and you get all of  the important core ideas and information  from that book it's perfect  if you don't have enough time to do all  the reading you want to do or if you  just want to sample a book before you  buy the entire thing I recently read a  brief history of time of course by the  great Stephen Hawking this is a book  that I have been aware of for so long  and other things got in the way and it  was fantastic to check it out on blinkin  blink east has books on politics  philosophy science they have 27  different nonfiction categories and a  subscription is only about eight bucks a  month and you get the entire library but  you can try it totally free and get 25%  off a subscription when you go to blink  is comm slash Pakman I've put the link  right underneath this video

